        "IQS_05": {
            "question_id": "IQS_05",
            "status": "scored",
            "score": 7,
            "description": "MongoDB's main competitive advantage in its defined business (managed document/operational database, particularly MongoDB Atlas) is the native document data model combined with a unified, multi-modal platform (document + vector search + search + time-series + streaming + transactional) tightly integrated across AWS/Azure/GCP. Evidence supporting durability:\n1) Document model + MQL: natively JSON/BSON-based, vs. PostgreSQL's bolted-on JSONB; reduces ORM overhead and speeds developer iteration. Reported 20-40% dev-time savings in case studies; 30M+ developers and 2.5M MongoDB University MAUs create network effects that are hard to replicate cheaply.\n2) Native horizontal sharding at the engine level; the company's own materials note that Postgres 'does not natively support horizontal sharding' and hyperscaler Postgres-compatible offerings are typically proprietary and lock customers in. MongoDB 8.0 improved resharding time ~50% and added Queryable Encryption (GA in 8.2) with range/prefix/suffix/substring queries — a feature peers do not match.\n3) Atlas runs in 85+ regions across all three major clouds with consistent operational experience; multi-cloud Atlas deployments grew ~72% YoY in 2024 and Atlas ARR reached ~$1.4B (~69.5% of revenue) by FY2025, with 58,300+ Atlas customers in Q2 FY2026. Lock-in is reinforced by 70% of Atlas ARR now using ≥1 additional capability (5x ARR vs. single-capability users).\n4) Recognized Leader in Forrester Wave for Translytical Data Platforms (Q4 2024), highest scores in 9 criteria; holds ~44-45% document-database market share (VMR, IIM), well ahead of Couchbase (~7%) and DocumentDB (~12.6%).\n\nKey limitations / evidence of erosion that cap the score below 10:\n- Hyperscaler 'embrace-and-extend' is real and intensifying: Azure Cosmos DB holds 16.5% managed-NoSQL mindshare (vs. MongoDB Atlas 12.6%, Enterprise Advanced 11.0% per PeerSpot Feb 2026); Microsoft donated DocumentDB to the Linux Foundation in 2025 with AWS/Google/Snowflake backing — a coordinated, well-funded open-source alternative to MongoDB's SSPL.\n- MongoDB Enterprise Advanced NoSQL mindshare fell from 23.3% to 16.9% YoY; gross margin slipped to 73% from 75% in FY25, indicating price pressure.\n- PostgreSQL (with JSONB, pgvector, Citus) is increasingly viewed by developers as 'good enough' for many document/vector workloads, threatening the developer-mindshare moat — though MongoDB argues the document model is native, not bolted-on.\n- Atlas mindshare (PeerSpot) grew from 1.1% to 10.9%–14.9% over the past year, but it remains well behind Cosmos DB in absolute share, and the company is still GAAP-unprofitable with heavy R&D (~35-40% of revenue) needed just to keep pace.\n\nOverall, the moat is real and supported by quantifiable evidence (market share, ARR, developer base, unique features like Queryable Encryption, multi-cloud footprint), but it is partially replicable by well-funded hyperscalers using bundling and open-source proxies, and by PostgreSQL for lower-end workloads. Hence a score of 7, not higher.",
